      "text": "This is an arctangent addition formula, which shows that\n\\[f(x) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}.\\]Therefore, $f(x)$ takes on the value $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ at $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, is not defined at $\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}$, and is always defined for all other values of $x.$\n\nTo show that $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ is an upper bound, note that\n\\[\\left|\\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\right| = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\\]By the addition formula for trigonometric functions,\n\\[\\left(\\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\right) + \\left(\\arctan x - \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\right) = 0.\\]To show that $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ is achieved, we may use related angle subtraction formulas to show the following,\n\\[2 \\arctan \\left(\\frac{2z - 1}{1 + z}\\right) + \\arctan \\frac{z}{1 - z} = \\arctan \\frac{3z}{1 - z^2} + 2 \\arctan \\left(\\frac{z}{1 - z}\\right),\\]where $z \\in [-1, 1] \\setminus \\{0\\}.$  Setting $z = x$ and the $z = \\frac{1}{1 - x}$, we find,\n\\[\\arctan \\frac{3x}{1 - x^2} = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}.\\]Therefore, $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ is the largest value that $f(x)$ is guaranteed to achieve. $\\Box$\n\nSo, $f(x) = \\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}.$ The answer is: \\frac{\\pi}{4}",
