      "text": "To solve for all numbers in the range of \\( f(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\), we need to simplify this expression and find the range of the resulting function.\n\nFirst, recall the identity for the sum of arctangents:\n\\[ \\arctan A + \\arctan B = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{A + B}{1 - AB} \\right) \\]\nprovided \\( AB < 1 \\).\n\nLet \\( A = x \\) and \\( B = \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\). Then we have:\n\\[ AB = x \\cdot \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = \\frac{x(1 - x)}{1 + x} = \\frac{x - x^2}{1 + x}. \\]\nWe need to determine if \\( AB < 1 \\):\n\\[ \\left| \\frac{x - x^2}{1 + x} \\right| < 1. \\]\nSince the expression is defined for all \\( x \\) (because the denominator \\( 1 + x \\) is never zero and is always positive), we can ignore the absolute value. We need:\n\\[ \\left| \\frac{x - x^2}{1 + x} \\right| < 1, \\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[ |x - x^2| < |1 + x|. \\]\nWe can consider the cases where \\( x \\geq 0 \\) and \\( x < 0 \\).\n\n1. **For \\( x \\geq 0 \\):**\n\\[ |x - x^2| < x + 1. \\]\nSince \\( x \\geq 0 \\), the left side is \\( x - x^2 \\leq 0 \\), so we have:\n\\[ -x + x^2 < x + 1 \\implies x^2 < 2x + 1 \\implies x^2 - 2x + 1 < 0 \\implies (x-1)^2 < 1 \\implies -1 < x < 2. \\]\nSince \\( x \\geq 0 \\), we get \\( 0 \\leq x < 2 \\).\n\n2. **For \\( x < 0 \\):**\n\\[ |x - x^2| < -x + 1. \\]\nSince \\( x < 0 \\), the left side is \\( x - x^2 \\), and we need:\n\\[ -x + x^2 < -x + 1. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ x^2 < 1 \\implies -1 < x < 1. \\]\nSince \\( x < 0 \\), we get \\( -1 < x < 0 \\).\n\nCombining these results, we see that the range of \\( f(x) \\) is \\( -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\). The expression \\( \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\) can take any value in this range, and for \\( x = 0 \\), the value is:\n\\[ f(0) = \\arctan 0 + \\arctan 1 = 0 + \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}. \\]\nTherefore, the range of \\( f(x) \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\left( -\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}. 